A destructor is declared by prefixing the class name with a tilde (`~`).
```cpp
class MyClass {
public:
    ~MyClass() { // Destructor declaration
        // Cleanup code
    }
};
```
It is implicitly called when:
1. A local object goes out of scope.
2. A dynamically allocated object is deleted using `delete`.
3. A temporary object is destroyed.
4. The program terminates (for static/global objects).

```cpp
class IntArrayWrapper {
    int* data;
    size_t size;
public:
    IntArrayWrapper(size_t s) : size(s) {
        data = new int[size]; // Acquire resource
        // Potentially throw an exception here
    }
    ~IntArrayWrapper() {
        delete[] data; // Release resource
    }
    // ... other member functions
};

void func() {
    IntArrayWrapper arr(10); // Resource acquired
    // If an exception occurs here, arr's destructor is still called.
} // arr goes out of scope, destructor called, memory freed.
```
The destructor `~IntArrayWrapper()` guarantees `delete[] data` is called, preventing leaks.
